+# Custody audit of the existing benchmarks (REVIEWER-1)
+
+Auditor model: `claude-opus-5-5` (from the session's system prompt). Written 2026-10-09 in the cloud container
+(4 vCPU, 15 GB). Read-only on every other ref: branches were read with `git show` / `git archive` into a scratch
+directory; nothing outside `research/mycelic_e2e/custody/` was modified.
+
+Refs audited:
+
+| short name | ref | commit |
+|---|---|---|
+| IMPL | `claude/mycelic-implementation-vr034p` (clone `/home/user/ng-impl`, local branch `claude/friendly-mayer-y9f1vt`) | `f96f263` |
+| VNEXT | `origin/claude/vnext-accuracy-70-percent-gsjg8m` | `4fc504a` |
+| MAIN | `origin/main` | `29307f2` |
+
+## 0. The question and the short answer
+
+**Hypothesis under test:** the existing benchmarks bypass ingestion, the per-holder NeuralGraph stores, hypergraph
+routing, or other core runtime pieces.
+
+**Verdict: confirmed for every accuracy number that has been published.** No published accuracy number
+(Mycelic v3/v4 found 0.625/0.713, the live 0.650 vs A2 0.875, LoCoMo 72.2%/42.5%) executes a single line of the
+IMPL `mycelic/` package. The only two harnesses that do execute IMPL code (`research/ingest_bench`, the seed
+scenario) are a one-holder storage microbenchmark and a functional pass/fail check with a deterministic fake model.
+Neither produces an accuracy number.
+
+**Separate finding: there is no hypergraph to bypass.** `git grep -il 'hypergraph\|hyperedge\|hyper-graph'` returns
+**0 files** (any type, not only `.py`) on IMPL, VNEXT and MAIN. So "hypergraph routing" is neither benchmarked nor
+implemented on any of the three refs. Routing in IMPL is coordinator question routing to holders. Routing in the
+simulator is a coin flip (see §1).
+
+### Component matrix
+
+Legend: **Y** = the real component executes; **sim** = a numpy stand-in with the same name; **N** = absent.
+
+| benchmark family | real connector / ingestion | per-holder NeuralGraph stores | coordinator authz / routing | inquiry / discovery loop | commit gate | hypergraph | class |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| research/mycelic E1–E12, vNext v3/v4/v5 (VNEXT, MAIN) | N | N (users are rows of a numpy record array) | sim (routing = `rng.random() > route_quality`) | sim | N | N | **simulation-only** |
+| research/mycelic/live (VNEXT) | N (the generator's rendered template text goes straight to the LLM) | N | sim (same simulator code) | sim | N | N | **partial-path** (live extraction operator only, inside the simulator) |
+| research/ingest_bench (IMPL) | **Y** (`local_export` connector → pipeline) | **Y**, one holder (`EvidenceStore` over `NeuralGraph.chat_memory`) | N | N | N | N | **component-only** |
+| seed scenario `python -m mycelic scenario` (IMPL) | Y for check m only (GitHub/Slack connectors against offline mocks). Checks a–l call `EvidenceStore.ingest_document` directly. | **Y** (2 separate holder processes and 8 embedded holders) | **Y** (HTTP API, real login) | **Y** (worker, loop) | **Y** (discovery engine → `knowledge.commit_claim`, see §4) | N | **end-to-end functional, synthetic** (13 checks; not an accuracy benchmark) |
+| LoCoMo `research/benchmarks/runner.py` (MAIN) | N (the benchmark builds `NeuralNode`s itself) | N for IMPL. It uses `NeuralGraph.research.retrieval` (`InMemoryNeuralGraphStorage` + Tesseract), a different codebase from the `NeuralGraph.chat_memory` that IMPL's holders use. | N | N | N | N | **component-only** (a different retriever from the one IMPL uses) |
+
+## 1. research/mycelic simulator: E1–E12 and the vNext v3 / v4 / v5 rounds
+
+**Entry commands** (VNEXT `research/mycelic/README.md`, `run_suite.sh`, `final_rerun.sh`):
+
+```sh
+python3 -m research.mycelic.experiments e1 ... e12        # run_suite.sh:7 loops e1b..e10
+sh research/mycelic/final_rerun.sh question_frac=0.65:qf_v3 batched_descent=true:v3_H link_time=hybrid:refit_hyb \
+   local_reextract=false:rx_reextract strict_targeting=false:cal_screen triage_target_chains=none:cal_screen
+python3 -m research.mycelic.quick_paired ... / arm_paired  # the quick_v4_heldout.jsonl evidence behind v4 0.713
+```
+
+**What it executes (import graph).** Every module in `research/mycelic/*.py` imports only siblings (`from .runner`,
+`.systems`, `.ops`, `.corpus`, `.org`, `.evalm`, `.models`) plus numpy, the standard library, matplotlib and markdown.
+Examples:
+* `experiments.py:19-25` imports `models`, `evalm`, `org`, `ops`, `runner` and `systems`;
+* `runner.py:14-20` imports `models`, `corpus`, `evalm`, `ops`, `org` and `systems`;
+* `systems.py:25-30` imports `corpus`, `models`, `org` and `ops`.
+
+A case-insensitive grep over all of `research/mycelic/**/*.py` on VNEXT for
+`neural_?graph|tesseract|hypergraph|hyperedge|holder|coordinator|commit.?gate|connector|ingest` matches only a
+label string, `loss_report.py:24`, "descent reached a facet holder". The simulator therefore imports nothing
+from `mycelic/` (IMPL) or from `NeuralGraph/`. The README says so itself: "The simulator and every generator need only
+numpy" and "Model tiers are a capability axis, not checkpoints. No GPU or local inference was available"
+(VNEXT `research/mycelic/README.md`, "Three things to know").
+
+**What stands in for each runtime piece:**
+* **Ingestion:** none. `corpus.build_corpus` generates a numpy record array, and `ops.extract` (`ops.py:233-241`)
+  reads the true fields: `t = rec["t"]`, `pol = rec["polarity"]`, `sig = rec["event"]`.
+* **Holders:** none. Users are row groups in that array (`systems.user_extract`, `systems.py:186`).
+* **Routing:** a coin. `systems.py:770`: `if self.rng.random() > tier.route_quality and len(scored) > 1:` drops the
+  best child. This is ORACLE_AUDIT row O5.
+* **Synthesis and verification:** coins at tier probabilities (`systems.py:1572`, `if self.rng.random() <
+  tier.causal_check`; ORACLE_AUDIT O3).
+* **Commit gate, authz:** none.
+
+**Gold / oracle exposure.** The relevant document is `docs/mycelic_v5/ORACLE_AUDIT.md` (VNEXT).
+* §1 states that the benchmark "depends on oracle-assisted simulation through its operators". The extraction
+  operator reads the true tuple, copies day and polarity exactly at every tier, and hands every system the
+  **true event id** as its near-duplicate signature for 92% of records (O1, O1a-c, lines 159-162).
+* The rankers are **fitted on simulator gold labels** (O9, line 171).
+* It lists three decision-level shortcuts, S1-S3. S1 is the A/A2 lexical prefilter keyed on the true `pred`; S3 is
+  hierarchy routing on the `hallucinated` flag.
+* The audit's own conclusion: "the sign of H vs A2 under live operators is not established by the simulator."
+
+**Verified by me:**
+1. **S1 is in the code as described.** VNEXT `systems.py:1616-1631` `_lexical_index`:
+   `key = surface_pred(corpus) if _ops.OBSERVABLE["lexical_index"] else corpus.recs["pred"]`. The legacy path keys the
+   index on the hidden true predicate. `ops.py:73` now defaults `OBSERVABLE["lexical_index"]` to True (protocol
+   amendment A1).
+2. **O1 is in the code as described.** `ops.py:239-241` copies the true `t`, `polarity` and `event` into the
+   extraction result.
+3. **The leakage suite runs as claimed.** `python3 -m unittest research.mycelic.test_leakage` on an extracted VNEXT
+   snapshot gave **"Ran 33 tests in 17.164s, OK (expected failures=4)"**. This matches the last `ledger_v5.jsonl`
+   row ("33 tests OK, 4 expected failures"). The audit text itself says 32 tests and 5 expected failures, which
+   was before P1 was fixed.
+
+**Published headline numbers.** All are simulated:
+* v3 (frozen vNext) found **0.625**, and v4 **0.713 [0.678, 0.750]** on 10k seeds 15-29. Source: `ledger_v5.jsonl`
+  row `r1-final`; status PDF table §1.
+* On the same runs, A2 scored 0.745 at 10k and 0.797 at 50k.
+* The head-to-head at 50k (VNEXT `docs/MYCELIC_ENTERPRISE.md` §1): A2 found **78%**, H_mycelic_full **57%**.
+* v5: the ledger holds only audit rows (`v5-audit-S1..gate`). No v5 accuracy number exists.
+
+**Why these numbers are not comparable to an E2E measurement of IMPL:**
+* No IMPL code runs.
+* "Holders", "routing", "verification" and "models" are numpy arrays and coins.
+* Extraction is oracle-assisted: exact days, exact polarity, true event ids.
+* The world is a 50-predicate closed schema with template text.
+* IMPL has no 600-entry register, no `found_anywhere_in_register` metric, and no planted-pattern gold.
+
+The simulator answers a question about an abstract architecture. It is not evidence about the implementation.
+
+**Classification: simulation-only.**
+
+MAIN carries the older (v3, PR #9) copy of the same simulator. `git ls-tree origin/main -- research/mycelic` lists
+no `live/`, no `test_leakage.py` and no `PROTOCOL_V5.md`, and the import structure is the same. Same classification.
+
+## 2. research/mycelic/live (VNEXT): Qwen3-1.7B edge agents
+
+**Entry command:** `python3 -m research.mycelic.live.run --stage {smoke,400,2000,10000} --edge-url ... [--kernel-url ...]`
+(`live/run.py:400-440`; README stage table).
+
+**What it executes:**
+* `live/run.py:56-68` imports the simulator itself: `ops`, `corpus`, `evalm`, `models`, `org`,
+  `runner.build_world/run_arch`, and `systems._lexical_index/chunked_long_context/user_extract`.
+* The world comes from `build_world(scale, seed)` (`run.py:462`).
+* The LLM's claims replace the extraction operator only (`run.py:564-566`):
+  `live_ul = user_extract(..., ex=live_ex)`, injected via `dataclasses.replace(world, _ul_cache=...)`.
+* Every system then runs through the unchanged simulator, `run_arch(name, live_world, alloc, seed)` (`run.py:280`).
+* The paired "sim" row comes from `run_arch(twin, world, ...)` (`run.py:269`).
+
+`live/README.md:47-56` says so explicitly. Still simulated are:
+* kernel synthesis checks;
+* hallucination injection;
+* abstraction coins (O4), routing and question-targeting coins (O5, O6);
+* rankers fitted on simulator gold;
+* the cost meter.
+
+The same README states that a live-vs-sim difference "measures **the extraction operator only**". The notes the
+model reads are the generator's template renderings (`notes.NoteStore` renders `Corpus.text`). They are not
+connector input.
+
+**Runtime pieces invoked:**
+* none of: IMPL ingestion, IMPL holders, coordinator, inquiry, commit gate, hypergraph.
+* "Routing" and "descent" are the simulator's coins.
+
+**Gold exposure.**
+* `live/score.py` is declared the only ground-truth reader (`live/README.md:65`), guarded by `test_leakage`'s
+  static scan and `test_live.py`'s scramble test. I did not run `test_live` (it needs no server, but time was
+  spent on `test_leakage`).
+* The downstream pipeline still has the simulator's oracle-assisted decisions: O3 coins executed on true
+  fields, and O9 gold-fitted rankers.
+* A2_live reads notes through the observable prefilter. That prefilter is exact on this generator's text
+  (ORACLE_AUDIT §2, "Why S1 and S3 measure zero": 100% agreement), so A2's recall rests on a perfect keyword
+  retriever.
+
+**Published numbers** (`artifacts/live/stage_400_s702.md`, `_A2_matched.md`; status PDF §1), all from 1 world,
+400 users, seed 702, 40 patterns:
+
+| system | live | sim |
+|---|---|---|
+| H_mycelic_full | **0.650** | 0.550 |
+| H_mycelic_prev | 0.575 | 0.600 |
+| B4 | 0.450 | 0.575 |
+| A2_live (same model family, 4B reader) | **0.875** | 0.925 |
+
+Also published: the smoke edge exact-tuple precision 0.822 and recall 0.801, and negations read **2 of 43**
+(`live/RESULTS.md` §2).
+
+**Not comparable to IMPL E2E.** Only extraction is real, and everything after extraction is the simulator.
+`RESULTS.md` §3-§4 are still "(filled in when the run completes)". The 350/429-agent seed-705 run named in
+ORCHESTRATION.md is unreachable and unpublished.
+
+**Classification: partial-path.** A real LLM is the extraction operator inside the simulator.
+
+## 3. research/ingest_bench (IMPL): ingestion and sharding microbenchmark
+
+**Entry command:** `python research/ingest_bench/run.py [--scale 0.2|1|4] [--out ...]` (`run.py:19-23`; RESULTS.md
+"How to run"). Note: the default `--out` is `results/latest.json` inside the repo (`run.py:191`).
+
+**What it executes (real IMPL code):**
+* Imports, `run.py:52-56`: `mycelic.evidence.EvidenceStore`, `mycelic.ingest.connectors.register_builtin`,
+  `mycelic.ingest.pipeline.IngestPipeline`, `mycelic.ingest.registry.ConnectorRegistry`,
+  `mycelic.ingest.service.IngestService`.
+* One holder, `EvidenceStore(path, holder_id="hold_bench", ...)` (`run.py:87`).
+* `svc.add_connector("local_export", ...)`, then `discover_sources`, `pipe.sync`, `pipe.process_available`
+  (`run.py:96-103`).
+* Retrieval goes through `store.search(..., audience=OWNER_AUDIENCE)` (`run.py:123-127`).
+* Sharding goes through `store.shards.start_split` (`run.py:215, 233`).
+
+`EvidenceStore` is built on `NeuralGraph.chat_memory`:
+* `mycelic/evidence/service.py:40` imports `MemoryRetriever`, and `:341` constructs it;
+* `mycelic/ingest/shards.py:38-39` imports `MemoryRetriever` and `ChatMemoryStore`.
+
+So the per-holder NeuralGraph store **is** exercised, for one holder.
+
+**Not invoked:** a second holder, the coordinator and authz (the audience is a hard-coded owner dict, `run.py:62`),
+inquiry, discovery, the commit gate, any hypergraph, and any model. The embedder is `hash-256`; there is no LLM.
+
+**Gold exposure:**
+* Each export file's source header carries `"domains": [dom]` (`corpus.py:111`), so domain routing is fed the
+  label by construction. The doc says so (`corpus.py:6-7`).
+* Known-item queries are 4 content words sampled from the target record (`corpus.py:92-97`).
+
+These are design choices for a storage benchmark, not leaks into an accuracy claim.
+
+**Reproduced here** (scale 0.2, 50 queries, output written to scratch, not to the repo): the run completed in
+9.5 s. Sharded recall@10 agreement (channel fusion) was 0.77, and known-item hit@10 (rrf) was 1.0 single-store vs
+0.94 sharded. The run therefore executes, and the sharded-vs-single agreement gap has the same direction as the
+published runs.
+
+**Published numbers** (`RESULTS.md`; scale 1, 2,995 records):
+
+| measure | single store | sharded |
+|---|---|---|
+| ingest throughput | 169.3 rec/s | 177.5 rec/s |
+| query p50 | 5.6 ms | 15.7 ms |
+| recall@10 agreement (rrf) | reference | 0.3845 |
+| recall@10 agreement (channel fusion) | reference | 0.674 |
+| known-item hit@10 | 0.98 | 0.90 (rrf) / 0.985 (channel) |
+
+**Comparability.** These are valid measurements of one IMPL component (one holder's ingest and search) on a
+synthetic corpus with hash embeddings. They say nothing about multi-holder discovery quality.
+
+**Classification: component-only** (real code, synthetic data).
+
+## 4. Seed scenario: `python -m mycelic scenario` (IMPL)
+
+**Entry command:** `python -m mycelic scenario --data-dir <new dir>`; the same scenario runs in pytest via
+`mycelic/tests/test_scenario.py`.
+
+**What it executes:**
+* `scenario.py:35-40` imports `mycelic.runtime.build_runtime/build_worker`, `mycelic.transport`, and
+  `seed.demo.run_seed`.
+* Per its docstring (`scenario.py:1-16`), it starts the API in-process, launches Elin's and Marcus's holders as
+  **separate processes** with their own SQLite files and keys, runs the discovery worker, and drives every check
+  through HTTP with a real login.
+
+**Reproduced here:** 2026-10-09 05:37 UTC, data dir in scratch.
+* Result: **"13/13 checks passed in 11.5s"**, with `api=http`.
+* Holder `hold_demo_a` ran as pid 3879 with its own `evidence.db`.
+* The brief's "12/12" is outdated: `docs/mycelic/VERIFICATION.md:112` now says 13 of 13. Check **m**
+  (GitHub + Slack) was added with the ingestion merge.
+
+**How evidence gets in (ingestion custody):**
+* For checks a–l, the seed writes embedded holders' documents with `store.ingest_document(...,
+  domains=row["domains"])` (`mycelic/seed/demo.py:333`).
+* External holders get signed `ingest` envelopes over the transport (`demo.py:339-346`).
+* `EvidenceStore.ingest_document` uses the caller's domains when given:
+  `doc_domains = [...] if domains else list(classified["domains"])` (`mycelic/evidence/service.py`, about line 481).
+  So **the seed's hand-assigned domains override the classifier**, and the **connector pipeline (`IngestPipeline`)
+  is not on the path for a–l**.
+* Only check **m** goes through real connectors, created via `POST /api/holders/{hid}/connectors`
+  (`scenario.py:1020-1025`) against **offline mocks** (`scenario.py:1000-1004`). The check's own evidence string
+  says "Mocks only: NOT a live verification of GitHub or Slack" (`scenario.py:1101`).
+
+**Model:** the deterministic fake (`mycelic/models/fake.py:1-11`). Its rules are lexical: shared tokens, numbers,
+negation words. The questions, documents and fake rules were written together, so passing the checks shows the
+plumbing works (authz 403/404, process separation, lineage, idempotent replay). It does not show discovery quality.
+
+**Commit gate:** on the path. Every discovery claim is committed through
+`self.knowledge.commit_claim(...)`, which returns `(claim, gate)` (`mycelic/discovery/engine.py:646`, and `:1108`
+for unrelated findings). The gate itself is in `mycelic/knowledge/gate.py`. Check d's supported discoveries can
+therefore only exist after the gate ran, and check m's docstring relies on the gate's independent-source counting
+(`scenario.py:995`). No check targets gate rejections specifically.
+
+**Hypergraph:** none (0 files on any ref).
+
+**Gold exposure:** not applicable as an accuracy benchmark. Pass criteria are structural (counts of roots,
+HTTP codes), fixed against a hand-written fixture.
+
+**Published number:** 13/13 checks (previously 12/12). This is a functional pass count, not a rate over a
+population.
+
+**Classification: end-to-end functional, synthetic.** It is the only harness that runs ingestion → holders →
+coordinator/authz → inquiry/discovery → commit gate together, but with a fake model, hand-set domains (a–l), mocked SaaS (m), and
+binary checks.
+
+## 5. LoCoMo harness: research/benchmarks (MAIN)
+
+**Entry commands** (MAIN `research/README.md:69-90`):
+
+```sh
+RUN_NAME=capstone ONLY_CONV=4,5,6,7,8,9 RETRIEVAL_MODE=local_pairs OPEN_DOMAIN_KEEP_CONTEXT=1 \
+  OPEN_DOMAIN_FORCE_INFER=2 OPEN_DOMAIN_INFER_WORLD=1 python research/benchmarks/runner.py
+python research/benchmarks/rescore.py
+MODE=combined python research/benchmarks/two_agent_eval.py; python research/benchmarks/retrieval_eval.py
+```
+
+**What it executes:**
+* MAIN `research/benchmarks/runner.py:17-31` imports `NeuralGraph.research.retrieval` (`NeuralNode`,
+  `InMemoryNeuralGraphStorage`, `tesseract`, `dialogue_linker`, `answering`, `reranker`, `service`).
+* `runner.py:545` loads `research/datasets/locomo10.json`.
+* Nodes are built in-process with `created_at=datetime.now()` (`runner.py:467, 651`).
+* Retrieval is `active_tesseract.retrieve(...)` (`runner.py:769`).
+
+IMPL's holder store uses a **different** library, `NeuralGraph.chat_memory` (`mycelic/evidence/service.py:38-40`,
+`mycelic/ingest/shards.py:38-39`). So LoCoMo measures the Tesseract research retriever, not anything IMPL runs.
+
+**Runtime pieces invoked:** none of: IMPL ingestion, holders, coordinator, inquiry, commit gate, hypergraph.
+
+**Gold exposure, verified by me:**
+* `runner.py:745-753` uses the gold `category` label to **select** questions. Category 5 (adversarial) is
+  skipped, and there is an `ONLY_CAT` filter.
+* `runner.py:858` gates the speaker-profile answer path on the gold label:
+  `if USE_SPEAKER_PROFILES and category == "single_hop" and not list_question:`. This contradicts the `# FAIR:
+  routing may only look at the question text, never the gold category label` comment 16 lines above (`:842`). It is
+  **off by default** (`runner.py:101`, `USE_SPEAKER_PROFILES ... == "1"`), so the shipped configuration is not
+  affected, but any run with `USE_SPEAKER_PROFILES=1` leaks the label.
+* `check_gold_in_memories_oracle` (`runner.py:179-215`) puts the gold answer into an LLM prompt. It is used for
+  recall@k scoring only (`runner.py:816-827`), not for answering.
+
+**Published numbers** (VNEXT status PDF §1):
+
+| judge | questions | score |
+|---|---|---|
+| Gemma, lenient (same family as the answerer) | 744 | **72.2%** |
+| Qwen, lenient | 584 | 65.6% |
+| Qwen, strict | 584 | **42.5%** |
+| substring match | 584 | 26.7% |
+
+The PDF itself notes that "Half the benchmark is unrun" and that "The same answer files score 13.5% to 64.9%
+depending only on the grader".
+
+**Not comparable to IMPL.** It uses a different store and retriever, a two-speaker conversational QA task, no
+holders, no authz, no discovery, and judge-dependent scoring.
+
